Bagels wrote:
> As I said previously, I'm gathering material for a paper on NetHack and 
> its community.  One of the things I'm finding that I'd like to focus on 
> is the twin questions of spoilers and save-scumming.
> 
> I know save-scumming is something of a touchy issue; what I'm 
> particularly curious about is how the anti-scumming policy came about, 
> and what folks' stances are on it.  I believe I've even seen some folks 
> mention that they play exclusively on NAO to avoid the possible 
> temptation of scumming.
> 
> As for spoilers, the situation is the reverse, from what I can tell: 
> it's generally socially acceptable to ask for help or use the spoilers 
> to succeed (or at least survive) in NetHack.  

The key distinction and underlying principle, I think, is that
these two policies (anti-savescum and pro-spoiler) are both policies
that in the long run maximize playing skill.

The nethack community is descended in part from the computer
hacking community, and as in that community, the thing that gives
people the most satisfaction is development of their own skills,
not development of an end product.  This is a principle that drives
many managers attempting to understand and motivate hackers
completely bonkers, btw, and leads to many books on how to manage
computer programmers, mostly written by people who Just Don't Get
It.  But I digress.

As others have pointed out, if you want into the high-score table
(an end product) all you have to do is edit the high-score table.
It's quick and easy.  Managers would just love it if programmers
could produce end products that quickly and easily, but the
programmers would not find it at all satisfying, because it does
not involve testing or advancing one's own skills.  Those who didn't
really care about it (the tradesmen as opposed to the hackers) would
probably stick with it as long as they were paid, but the hackers
would instantly leave this job, now reduced to the point of being
boring, to them.

Knowledge contributes to playing skills, thus tolerance of
spoilers; experience has shown that playing without savescumming
contributes more to playing skills than playing with savescumming,
hence intolerance of savescumming.

In both cases, people whose internal bias is the advancement of
skills have determined the choice most satisfying to them. People
whose internal biases are different will naturally reach different
choices.

But people whose internal bias is the advancement of skills tend
to find people whose internal biases are otherwise to be at best
boring, and more usually annoying.  And that is why you get a
certain amount of cultural reinforcement here; ostracizing such
people, even to the extent of open despite, is an instinctive
defense against having boring or annoying people "take over" the
subculture.

> The situation reminds me 
> greatly of something that's been discussed in the class I'm writing this 
> paper for - James Paul Gee's concept of "distributed knowledge" diffused 
> within a community.  NetHack seems to be almost impossible to win by 
> oneself - it's a single-player game that's almost too complicated for 
> any player acting entirely alone to figure out.  

It is the exception rather than the rule, but it has been done,
from time to time.  The mantra for beginning nethackers is "die
and learn."  A first ascension by a player who has *not* got
spoilers from the community happens occasionally, but usually
represents knowledge gleaned from tens of thousands of prior
games ending with character deaths.

